   $q$Which revision of sentence 14 best concludes the report by connecting to both how locks work and why the canal was important?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Today, the Calder Canal has 22 locks and climbs nearly 60 meters between two towns.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"restates-detail"},{"text":"Today, the Calder Canal carries mostly tour boats and kayaks, which can be rented in Wexley.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"introduces-new-detail"},{"text":"Today, the Calder Canal is the most interesting place to visit in the whole region.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"opinion-not-conclusion"},{"text":"Today, the canal carries mostly tour boats and kayaks, but its 22 locks still lift them with nothing more than gravity and gates, the simple method that once made the canal vital for trade.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Today, the canal carries mostly tour boats and kayaks, but its 22 locks still lift them with nothing more than gravity and gates, the simple method that once made the canal vital for trade.$q$, $q$The correct revision recalls the gravity-powered process from paragraphs 2 and 3 and the canal's role in trade from paragraph 4. The version listing 22 locks and 60 meters only repeats facts without drawing the ideas together.$q$, 'draft', '9aa3e835-3388-5d59-9d92-569aaddb4caa')
