   $q$Read the group of words: "The volunteers who walked the dogs at the animal shelter every Saturday morning." Which revision turns it into a complete sentence?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The volunteers who walked the dogs at the animal shelter every Saturday morning earned a service award.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The volunteers walking the dogs at the animal shelter every Saturday morning.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"participle-fragment"},{"text":"Because the volunteers walked the dogs at the animal shelter every Saturday morning.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"subordinate-clause-fragment"},{"text":"The volunteers, who walked the dogs at the animal shelter every Saturday morning.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"relative-clause-fragment"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The volunteers who walked the dogs at the animal shelter every Saturday morning earned a service award.$q$, $q$The subject "volunteers" needs a main verb, and "earned" supplies it; "walked" belongs to the "who" clause. Adding commas around the "who" clause does not give the subject a main verb, so it is still a fragment.$q$, 'draft', null)
