   $q$Which choice about the farmers market is NOT a complete sentence?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Open every Sunday since spring, the farmers market sits on the corner of Fifth Avenue.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"introductory-phrase-misread-as-fragment"},{"text":"The farmers market on the corner of Fifth Avenue, open every Sunday since spring.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The farmers market on the corner of Fifth Avenue has been open every Sunday since spring.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"complete-sentence-misread-as-fragment"},{"text":"Visit the farmers market on Fifth Avenue this Sunday.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"imperative-misread-as-fragment"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The farmers market on the corner of Fifth Avenue, open every Sunday since spring.$q$, $q$"The farmers market ..., open every Sunday since spring" has a subject but no main verb, so it is a fragment. The sentence that begins "Open every Sunday" is complete, because "sits" is its main verb.$q$, 'draft', null)
