   $q$Read the group of words: "While the orchestra tuned their instruments and the audience found their seats in the crowded hall." Which revision makes it a complete sentence?$q$, $q$[{"text":"While the orchestra tuned their instruments and the audience found their seats in the crowded hall, and the conductor reviewed the score.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"conjunction-does-not-fix-fragment"},{"text":"While the orchestra tuned their instruments and the audience found their seats in the crowded hall, the conductor reviewed the score.","is_correct":true},{"text":"While the orchestra tuned their instruments and the audience found their seats in the crowded hall; the conductor reviewed the score.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"semicolon-after-dependent-clause"},{"text":"While the orchestra was tuning their instruments and the audience was finding their seats in the crowded hall.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"fragment-unfixed"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$While the orchestra tuned their instruments and the audience found their seats in the crowded hall, the conductor reviewed the score.$q$, $q$The "while" clause is dependent, so it needs a main clause after a comma, such as "the conductor reviewed the score." A semicolon cannot separate a dependent clause from the main clause it belongs to.$q$, 'draft', null)
