-- Passage wg9-writing-with-evidence-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('bd5f01da-1909-5142-a91a-1d328e26d8ef', 'wg9-writing-with-evidence-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g9-writing-with-evidence'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Draft: Warming Up the Right Way$q$, $q$(1) In "Move Before You Move," sports scientist Keiko Brandt describes a study of how warm-up routines affect young soccer players. (2) Brandt's team followed sixty high school players for one season and split them into two groups. (3) One group warmed up by holding still stretches, such as reaching for their toes for thirty seconds. (4) The other group used moving exercises like high knees, side shuffles and short sprints. (5) At the end of the season, players in the moving group sprinted an average of four percent faster and missed fewer practices because of sore or strained muscles. (6) ____

(7) Brandt also explains why moving warm-ups may work better. (8) Brandt writes that these exercises raise body temperature and heart rate, which prepares muscles for the quick starts and stops that soccer requires. (9) Still stretches, Brandt notes, relax the muscles instead of waking them up. (10) Soccer is the most widely played sport in the world. (11) ____, a warm-up that copies the motions of the game seems to prepare players more completely than one that keeps them standing still.

(12) In conclusion, Brandt's study suggests that coaches should replace still stretches with moving exercises before games and practices.$q$, 'draft')
