insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('281e851b-ca46-5e9b-a469-57b06e7317bb', 'wg10-argument-writing-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g10-argument-writing'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Repairable Laptops for Dunmore Schools$q$, $q$(1) The Dunmore School District replaces about 900 student laptops every year, most of them because of a single broken part such as a cracked screen or a worn-out battery. (2) When the district signs its next equipment contract in March, it should choose laptops designed with parts that can be replaced at school. (3) On the current models, a cracked screen costs $210 to fix through the manufacturer, which is nearly half the price of a new device. (4) On repairable models sold by several companies, a replacement screen costs about $45 and can be installed by a trained technician in under fifteen minutes. (5) Laptops can be used for many things, like writing essays and watching videos. (6) Fewer discarded devices would also reduce the electronic waste the district sends to recycling centers each year.

(7) Opponents argue that repairable laptops cost about $60 more per device up front. (8) This difference is real, and the district would need to plan for it. (9) The district's technology office estimates that repairing instead of replacing broken laptops would save roughly $95,000 a year, more than covering the higher purchase price within two years. (10) In addition, a pilot at Grayson Middle School trained eight students as repair assistants, giving them practical experience with electronics.

(11) To conclude, laptops are expensive, so whatever the district decides is fine.$q$, 'draft')
