   $q$Which sentence about a student radio station is punctuated correctly?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The student radio station broadcasts three shows; a morning news program, a jazz hour, and a call-in quiz. The quiz, however, is the most popular.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"semicolon-for-colon"},{"text":"The student radio station broadcasts: a morning news program, a jazz hour, and a call-in quiz; the quiz is the most popular.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"colon-after-verb"},{"text":"The student radio station broadcasts three shows: a morning news program, a jazz hour, and a call-in quiz, however, the quiz is the most popular.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"comma-splice-with-adverb"},{"text":"The student radio station broadcasts three shows: a morning news program, a jazz hour, and a call-in quiz. The quiz, however, is the most popular.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The student radio station broadcasts three shows: a morning news program, a jazz hour, and a call-in quiz. The quiz, however, is the most popular.$q$, $q$The correct sentence uses a colon after the complete clause "The student radio station broadcasts three shows" and sets off "however" with commas inside its own sentence. Ending the list with ", however, the quiz is the most popular" joins a new independent clause to the list with only commas around "however," which is a comma splice.$q$, 'draft', null)
