        '9-12', $q$Drinking Water from the Fog$q$, $q$(1) In the coastal hills above the village of Alto Serrano, rain falls on only a handful of days each year, yet thick fog rolls in from the ocean almost every morning. (2) For generations, residents carried their drinking water up the hillside from a distant well. (3) Today, many of them collect it directly from the air by using fog nets.

(4) A fog net is a large panel of fine plastic mesh stretched between two poles and turned to face the incoming wind. (5) As fog drifts through the mesh, tiny droplets strike the fibers, cling to them and merge into larger drops. (6) Gravity then pulls the drops into a gutter at the base of the net, and a pipe carries the water to a storage tank. (7) The mesh is often a dark green color that many visitors find attractive.

(8) In 2021, engineers from the Coastal Water Institute installed twenty-four nets on the ridge above Alto Serrano and recorded their output for one full year. (9) Each square meter of mesh collected an average of 6 liters of water per foggy day, which was enough for the village school to stop buying bottled water within three months.

(10) The nets have limits. (11) Strong winds can tear the mesh, and the nets produce almost nothing during the clear months of late summer. (12) In addition, the water must still be filtered before drinking because dust and bird droppings can collect on the fibers.

(13) Fog nets are a really cool idea.$q$, 'draft')
