insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('70c51dab-5890-5efc-95c4-2dc4c4fa8336', 'wg10-informative-writing-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g10-informative-writing'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Lichens as Living Air Monitors$q$, $q$(1) Lichens are the crusty, leafy or branching growths found on tree bark, stone walls and old fence posts. (2) Although a lichen looks like a single plant, it is actually a partnership between a fungus and an alga, two organisms that live together and depend on each other. (3) The fungus provides shelter and absorbs water, while the alga uses sunlight to make food for both.

(4) Because lichens have no roots, they take in water and minerals directly from rain and air. (5) As a result, they also absorb pollutants, especially sulfur dioxide, a gas released when coal and oil are burned. (6) Some species are extremely sensitive and die when pollution levels rise, while others can survive in dirtier air. (7) Scientists therefore use lichens as bioindicators, living things whose presence or absence reveals the condition of an environment.

(8) In 2019, students at Harlow Ridge High School surveyed the lichens on 60 trees across their city. (9) They found leafy and branching species on almost every tree in the city park but only crusty species on trees beside the highway. (10) Lichens come in many colors, including orange, gray and pale yellow. (11) The results matched air sensor data showing higher sulfur dioxide levels near the highway.

(12) In conclusion, lichens can be found in many places.$q$, 'draft')
