insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('6771b5d8-4a42-59dd-9981-734016f7941a', 'wg10-informative-writing-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g10-informative-writing'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Glowing Waters of Lucero Bay$q$, $q$(1) On moonless nights in Lucero Bay, every paddle stroke leaves a swirl of blue light in the dark water. (2) The glow comes from dinoflagellates, single-celled organisms that drift near the surface and produce light through a chemical reaction. (3) This ability, called bioluminescence, also occurs in fireflies, some fungi and many deep-sea fish.

(4) Inside each dinoflagellate, a molecule named luciferin reacts with oxygen with the help of an enzyme. (5) The reaction releases energy as light rather than heat, so the glow is cool to the touch. (6) The organisms flash only when the water around them is disturbed, which scientists believe startles their predators.

(7) Lucero Bay holds an unusually dense population of dinoflagellates for two reasons. (8) First, its narrow mouth limits how much water flows out to the open sea, so the organisms are not washed away. (9) Mangrove trees along the shore drop leaves that release the nutrients the dinoflagellates feed on. (10) A 2022 survey by the Island Marine Station counted up to 700,000 dinoflagellates in a single gallon of bay water.

(11) Kayaking is a fun activity for families on vacation. (12) Researchers warn that the glow may be in danger.$q$, 'draft')
