-- Passage wg10-informative-writing-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('74db9ea7-c0e0-5a8d-8c36-a9287c596dd6', 'wg10-informative-writing-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g10-informative-writing'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Storing Energy by Moving Water$q$, $q$(1) Solar panels and wind turbines produce electricity only when the sun shines or the wind blows, but people need power at all hours. (2) One way to bridge this gap is pumped storage hydropower, a system that stores energy by moving water uphill. (3) Engineers sometimes call such a system a "water battery."

(4) A pumped storage plant uses two reservoirs, one at the top of a hill and one at the bottom. (5) When electricity is plentiful and cheap, usually in the middle of a sunny day, pumps push water from the lower reservoir to the upper one. (6) When demand rises in the evening, operators release the water downhill through turbines, which spin generators and send power back to the grid. (7) The plant loses some energy in each cycle, but it typically returns about 80 percent of the electricity used for pumping.

(8) The Corrin Valley plant, completed in 2020, can supply about 300,000 homes for up to eight hours. (9) Building it required digging an upper reservoir as large as forty soccer fields. (10) Soccer is the most popular sport in the world.

(11) Pumped storage also has drawbacks. (12) It requires a site with a steep height difference and plenty of water, and construction can take more than five years.$q$, 'draft')
